Subject: Re: possible pkgsrc emacs framework improvements?
To: Thomas Klausner <wiz@NetBSD.org>

(DRAFT -- not sent.  Numbers to be filled in from the emacs30 sweep.)

Hi Thomas,

Yes, I would like to work on this.  I have a prototype of all three
and have built with it, so rather than say "sounds good" here is what
it does and what it costs.

Version selection.  modules.mk now does what lang/python does:
EMACS_VERSION_REQD picks one version and refuses if the package does
not accept it, so a bulk pass leaves it alone instead of building it
for something else; otherwise EMACS_VERSION_DEFAULT (EMACS_TYPE keeps
working, it is just the default now); otherwise the first version the
package accepts.  _EMACS_VERSIONS_ALL is now newest first so that
"first accepted" means the package's first choice, the way
PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED is written.

One thing I did not expect.  Choosing from EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED
alone is not safe: a package can name a version that has since been
removed.  After emacs21 went, fifteen packages in my tree still asked
for it, and picking emacs21 leaves _EMACS_PKGDIR empty and the include
dies with "Cannot open /version.mk" rather than failing cleanly.  So
the choice is made from the intersection of what the package accepts
and what pkgsrc still has, and when that is empty the package says so
and fails properly.  My first cut had this wrong and the sweep below
is what caught it.

Install location and name.  The lisp goes to
share/emacs/<version>/site-lisp, which Emacs already searches, and
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX is ${_EMACS_TYPE}-, so pcl-cvs is
emacs20-pcl-cvs and apel is emacs29-apel, emacs29nox-apel,
emacs30-apel and so on, as you described.

pbulk.  Three lines in mk/pbulk/pbulk-index.mk, the same shape as the
python and ruby entries.

What it costs.  Less than I feared.  Of the 106 packages that read
modules.mk, 85 of 98 PLISTs already use ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX} and 28 of
the 29 that pass a lisp directory to their build already pass
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}.  Six PLISTs write the path out by hand and want
changing: cqual, rsltc, tamago-tsunagi, mailutils, twittering-mode
and emacs-dict-client.

The part that cannot be found by reading is a package whose PLIST uses
the variable but whose install writes the path itself.  devel/pcl-cvs
is one: nothing in its Makefile says lispdir, and the bundled makefile
has lispdir = $(datadir)/emacs/site-lisp.  The PLIST follows the
variable, so the file check catches it -- building is the test.  I
built to find them.

<<SWEEP RESULTS>>

Happy to split this up however suits you, and to put the framework
part up first so it can be looked at on its own.

